Consider a tiling system $\mathcal{T}=(T,\sigma,1)$, the path assembly $P$ satisfies macroscopic initial conditions if:
\begin{itemize}
\item $P$ is finite;
\item $\dom{P} \cap \dom{\sigma}= \mathrm{pos}(P_1)$; 
\item $y_{(P_{|P|})}>\mathcal{B}^f$;
\item $P$ is simple.
\item $P$ satisfies the first quadrant convention and the axis convention.
\end{itemize}

\label{constraint:stakes}
Let $P$ be a simple path assembly producible by $\mathcal{T}=(T,\sigma,1)$ satisfying macroscopic initial conditions. Consider an index $1\leq s \leq |P|$, a vector $\ensuremath{\overrightarrow{v}}$ of $\mathbb{Z}^2$ and two path assemblies $\mathcal{S}^-$ and $\mathcal{S}^+$ producible by $\mathcal{T}$. The path assembly $P$ satisfies the \emph{stakes constraint} at index $s$ of direction $\ensuremath{\overrightarrow{v}}$ with path assemblies $\mathcal{S}^-$ and $\mathcal{S}^+$ if and only if :
\begin{itemize}
\item $y_{\ensuremath{\overrightarrow{v}}}\geq 1$ and $||\ensuremath{\overrightarrow{v}}|| \leq \mathcal{B}$;
\item $\mathcal{S}^- \cdot (P_{[s,|P|]}-\ensuremath{\overrightarrow{v}})$ and $\mathcal{S}^+ \cdot (P_{[s,|P|]}+\ensuremath{\overrightarrow{v}})$ are path assemblies producible by $\mathcal{T}$; 
\item $\sigma, \mathcal{S}^+, \mathcal{S}^-$ and $P_{[1,s]}$ belong to $\mathcal{R}(\mathcal{B}^s)$.
\end{itemize}
The square $\mathcal{R}(\mathcal{B}^s)$ is called the \emph{stakes zone} and $\mathcal{S}^-$, $\mathcal{S}^+$ are called stakes.

Figure \ref{fig:hunt:proof} illustrates the reasoning made along this proof. Consider a path assembly $P$ producible by  $\mathcal{T}=(T,\sigma,1)$ satisfying the macroscopic initial conditions and the stakes constraint at index $s$ according to vector $\ensuremath{\overrightarrow{v}}$ and an index $s \leq \ind{1} \leq |P|$. First if $P_{[\ind{1},|P|]}$ and $P_{[\ind{1},|P|]}-\ensuremath{\overrightarrow{v}}$ does not intersect then index $\ind{1}$ matches the \emph{jail} final case. Otherwise if $P_{[\ind{1},|P|]}$ and $P_{[\ind{1},|P|]}-\ensuremath{\overrightarrow{v}}$ intersect then either this intersection is a conflict (index $\ind{1}$ matches the \emph{conflict} final case) or there is an agreement between $P_{[\ind{1},|P|]}$ and $P_{[\ind{1},|P|]}-\ensuremath{\overrightarrow{v}}$. If this intersection is an agreement let $\ind{3}=\mathcal{H}(\ind{1})$ and by definition of $\ind{3}$, two cases can occurs either $\mathrm{pos}(P_{\ind{3}}-\ensuremath{\overrightarrow{v}}) \in \dom{P_{[\ind{1},\ind{3}]}}$ or $\mathrm{pos}(P_{\ind{3}}) \in \dom{P_{[\ind{1},\ind{3}]}-\ensuremath{\overrightarrow{v}}}$. In the first (resp. second) case, there exists $\ind{1} \leq \ind{2} < \ind{3}$ such that $P_{\ind{2}}=P_{\ind{3}}-\ensuremath{\overrightarrow{v}}$ (resp. $P_{\ind{2}}-\ensuremath{\overrightarrow{v}}=P_{\ind{3}}$) and then the segment $P_{[\ind{2},\ind{3}]}$ is a candidate segment and $\overrightarrow{P_{\ind{2}}P_{\ind{3}}} =\ensuremath{\overrightarrow{v}}$ (resp. $\overrightarrow{P_{\ind{2}}P_{\ind{3}}}=-\ensuremath{\overrightarrow{v}}$). Moreover, since $P_{[\ind{1},\ind{3}]}$ and $P_{[\ind{1},\ind{3}]}-\ensuremath{\overrightarrow{v}}$ intersect only at position $\mathrm{pos}(P_{\ind{2}})$ (resp. $\mathrm{pos}(P_{\ind{3}})$) and since $\ind{1}\leq \ind{2}$ then the segment $P_{[\ind{2},\ind{3}]}$ is also a good segment of $P$. Consider the case $\overrightarrow{P_{\ind{2}}P_{\ind{3}}} =\ensuremath{\overrightarrow{v}}$ (the other case is symmetric) then if the good segment $P_{[\ind{2},\ind{3}]}$ is pumpable then index $\ind{1}$ matches the \emph{pumpable} final case. Otherwise, let $Q=\omepluse{P_{[\ind{2},\ind{3}]}}$ and let $\ind{4}$ be the first conflict of the good segment $P_{[2,3]}$ of $P$. By lemma \ref{lem:good:cond}, we have $\mathrm{pos}(Q_{\ind{4}}) \in \dom{\sigma \cup P_{[1,\ind{2}-1]}}$. We consider the different cases:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\mathrm{pos}(Q_{\ind{4}}) \in \dom{P_{[s,\ind{2}-1]}}$, if $\dom{Q}\cap \mathcal{R}(\mathcal{B}^s)\neq \emptyset$ then index $\ind{1}$ matches the \emph{Pumping enters stakes zone} final case otherwise  index $\ind{1}$ matches the \emph{Conflict after stake} final case;
\item $\mathrm{pos}(Q_{\ind{4}}) \in \dom{\sigma \cup P_{[1,s]}}$: by definition of the stakes constraint, $\dom{\sigma \cup P_{[1,s]}}$ is a subset of the stakes zone and thus index $\ind{1}$ matches the \emph{Pumping enters stakes zone} final case.
\end{itemize}

